 "result": "\n\nUpdated. Summary of what changed and what I kept:\n\n**Changed** — dropped the explicit human-versus-bot contrast. The request is now:\n\n> \"Add a check reaction to the 2.4 rollout message in #releases — the human one that lena.hart@northwind.example has already reacted with 👀 to.\"\n\n\"The human one\" reads as the user's own description of the message (natural in a channel with a deploy bot posting rollout noise), not as an added tie-breaker. The condition is still exactly `A:User.is_bot` on the author (c3 text updated to \"the human one\"); `d3` still fails it and only it, so the fact is tested identically. No other part of the scenario changed.\n\n**Kept** — the full email in the request. The reader's instinct is right for a Slack-native user, but the brief's fact *is* `A:User.email`: the request must carry the address as the operative identifier, or the fact isn't tested (the method doc's own partial-identity guidance models requested emails: \"For `pat.kim@…`, use `pat.kimura@…`\"). In this world it's also the unambiguous choice — the directory has two \"Lena Hart\"s and a \"Lena Hartmann\", so a colleague who has the address on hand (directory, ticket) would write the email precisely because a bare name would be genuinely ambiguous here; a name-based request would even swallow the F1 decoy (the contractor is also named \"Lena Hart\"). I kept it as a plain identifier with no framing, which is the natural shorthand.",
